    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d328">

                <pb facs="0428" n="414" xml:id="pg414"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">501.BC/9–3048</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in India</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Donovan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">New Delhi</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-09-30">September 30,
                        1948</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">No. 1133</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Editorial in Delhi <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Statesman</hi>, September 30, regarding Hyderabad.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to New Delhi’s
                    telegram no. 896 of September 30, 1948<anchor n="1" xml:id="d328-f1"/> and to
                    enclose the full text of the editorial which appeared in the Delhi <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Statesman</hi> of September 30, 1948 entitled “The UN
                    and Hyderabad”.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d328-f2"/> The views expressed in this
                    editorial are shared by the Embassy. There is no question but that the Nizam,
                    surrounded by a small minority of Muslim extremists, was a menace to the peace
                    and security of India. The question whether Hyderabad was an independent state
                    can no doubt be argued for hours but the blunt facts are that India could not
                    permit a hostile government to function within its borders.</p>
                <p>The Embassy cannot too strongly emphasize that American interests in India would
                    best be served by considering the Hyderabad question as closed.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Howard Donovan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
